WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.206
"What Is Taurine in Energy Drinks and
Does It Enhance Cognitive or Athletic Performance?"

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.600
Energy drinks
are widely consumed

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.600
in the United States,
with sales exceeding

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.120
350 million gallons
(1.324 billion liters).

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.160
But there's only like
335 million people in the US,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.760
so, we each average more
than a gallon (3.8 L) a year?

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.640
Is it just the caffeine
that people are after?

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.840
A major ingredient
in popular energy drinks

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.240
is taurine.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.600
What is that?

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.600
Taurine is one
of the most abundant

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.680
free amino acids in our body,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.400
free as in
not incorporated into proteins.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.160
We make it in our own bodies,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.400
that's why it's
considered non-essential,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.960
as in we don't have
to get any in our diet.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.720
However, premature infants

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.640
are vulnerable
to taurine deficiency

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.800
because they initially lack
some of the enzymes needed

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.480
to synthesize taurine,

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.880
but breast milk provides
all the taurine they need,

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.480
and it's often added
to infant formula as well.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.640
We aren't the only
animals who make it.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.360
The name taurine derives
from the Latin taurus,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.640
meaning bull or ox,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.720
because taurine
was first isolated

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.480
from the bile of an ox in 1827,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.440
decades before
it was discovered in people.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.160
It's funny, in my search
of the medical literature,

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.320
I kept coming across
articles on taurine surgery.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.920
What does that mean?

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.600
That's what they call surgery
for bullfighting injuries.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.120
Anyway, taurine was
first isolated in the 1800s,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.360
but not much was known
about this molecule

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.920
until the 1990s,
after we found low levels

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.360
in preterm formula-fed infants.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:38.640
Even through taurine

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.400
isn't incorporated
into proteins,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.200
it plays important roles
throughout our body.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.280
Clues to its importance
include the fact

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.880
that our body has not one,
not two, but at least five

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.720
pathways to make it.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.400
And we make a lot of it.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.880
If you look down
at the bathroom scale

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.200
and it says 200 pounds (90 kg),

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.720
about one-fifth
of a pound (90g)

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.360
of that is straight taurine.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.480
Other clues can be found
in where it's made.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.440
The main pathway enzyme
is found not only in our liver

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.560
but in sensitive tissues
like the eye, brain, kidney,

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.000
breast milk-producing glands,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.840
and our reproductive organs.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.160
The levels in sperm
suggest a protective effect.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.200
Taurine is described
as a major antioxidant,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.800
but it's really
only good at squelching

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.560
one type of free radical,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.280
but that free radical
produces pro-aging toxins,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.800
like advanced glycation
end products,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.320
so, that squelching activity
is an important function.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.080
In addition to our brain,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.640
taurine is also found
concentrated in our muscles,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.040
where it's involved
in energy production.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.080
Presumably, that's why
it's put into energy drinks.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.200
Does taurine help

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.160
with cognitive
or physical performance?

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.880
Probably neither,
like in this study,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.440
no benefit for either
physical or cognitive ability,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.640
with some studies suggesting
taurine actually undercuts

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.640
some of caffeine's
beneficial effects

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:55.920
on cognition or vigor.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.720
Or that it helps with
one kind of cognitive task

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.280
but hurts
performance of another.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.640
Taurine may be more
red herring than Red Bull,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.960
as the human studies
often cited to support it

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.160
have been improperly designed

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.720
and lack
the appropriate controls.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:11.880
Put all the studies together,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:12.960
and there is little evidence

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.160
of taurine increasing
endurance exercise,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.920
and it's unclear whether
there are any sports benefits

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.200
like reducing muscle soreness
or speeding recovery.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.360
Taurine ingestion
might even be detrimental

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.600
to maximal muscle power
in noncaffeine consumers.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.440
The picture
is just really unclear,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.240
which is why I never
paid much attention to it

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.920
until, boom,
this study came out,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.080
suggesting taurine deficiency
was a driver of aging.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.000
It was published after
I had already submitted

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.760
the manuscript
for my longevity book,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.040
How Not to Age;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.040
so, I've been eager
to dive into the topic

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.600
to see if there was anything
there.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.840
As we age,
the taurine levels

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.560
in our body fall
by as much as 80 percent.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.400
This may be due
to the depletion

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.440
of the taurine-synthesizing
enzyme in our liver,

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.120
suggesting we may need to start

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.560
getting more taurine
in our diet as we age.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.840
The reason it was concluded

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.160
that taurine
was a driver of aging

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.520
is because,
when attempts were made

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.680
to restore youthful levels
in animals

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.080
through taurine supplementation,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.480
this reversal
of taurine deficiency

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.360
increased the health
of worms, rodents, and primates,

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.400
and increased the lifespan
of worms and rodents.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.785
We'll dig into
this important study, next.

